WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:14.860
THE HATE FOR PAYPAL:
PayPal is by far one of the most functional

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.119
apps in the world.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:22.769
We use it to pay rent, private teachers, contractors,
freelancers, friends, family, and even certain

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:23.769
purchases.

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:28.730
PayPal made sending and receiving money through
the internet more straight forward than ever

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.750
before.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.500
All you needed was someone’s email address.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.980
You didn’t need to deal with bank accounts
or routing numbers or any of that.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.890
Just make sure you typed in their email correctly
and hit send.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.890
Ironically, this is not what PayPal was actually
created for.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.780
The founders envisioned that PayPal would
be an internet based financial management

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.469
system, and sending money using email addresses
was just a side feature.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:56.460
Yet, it quickly became the main feature.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.160
We can’t forget about Venmo either who PayPal
acquired back in 2013.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:07.370
In the US, Venmo is so popular for splitting
bills that it has become a verb like Google.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.049
Considering the popularity and functionality
of PayPal, I don’t think you’d be surprised

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:16.420
to hear that PayPal boasts a market cap of
$100 billion.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.390
And that’s with a 75% sell off.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.229
But, what is surprising to me though is that
people aren’t exactly fans of PayPal.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:27.900
In fact, PayPal has a quite a negative reputation.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.600
Several months ago, I posted a video about
the downfall of PayPal stock, and most of

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.409
the comments were actually happy that PayPal
was getting destroyed.

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:39.799
“Paypal is old tech and its total garbage.”

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:42.460
“I hope this company goes bankrupt.”

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.850
“Good I hate that company.”

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.979
“PayPal is a terrible company that I only
deal with when I have to.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:51.020
Glad there are other options now.”

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:55.200
It’s not just my YouTube comments that were
so critical of PayPal either.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.509
After a little digging, I quickly realized
that people don’t just have qualms with

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:00.509
PayPal.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:03.350
They actually utterly despise the company.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.750
Take TrustPilot for example.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:13.230
They have a solid 23,000 reviews, but 87%
of those reviews are literally 1 star, and

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.140
only a mere 8% are 5 star.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.830
But, how did such a useful and seemingly neutral
company earn such a terrible reputation?

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:28.209
SIDING WITH SCAMMERS:
One of the most common use cases of PayPal

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:31.920
is to pay third party sellers that you don’t
fully trust.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.530
If the seller does try to pull a quick on
you, you would hope that PayPal would side

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.349
with the customer and help facilitate a refund.

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:44.540
But, according to the vast majority of online
anecdotes, PayPal never sides with the customer,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.769
even when they’re are several red flags
and a bunch of evidence.

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:50.540
Take RedHat27’s experience for example.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.240
RedHat tried to buy one of these Wyze security
cameras from a third party reseller using

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.010
PayPal.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:00.409
As you would guess, RedHat would never receive
his camera, so he would file a claim with

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:03.200
PayPal and do some digging on his own.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.000
First, he tried calling the seller’s phone
number, and the person that picked up was

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.640
some tow truck driver.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.790
Apparently, the tow truck driver was receiving
over 300 calls every day from people who have

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.110
fallen for this scam, and he had already reported
it to the police and the Better Business Bureau.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.550
You would think that this information alone
was enough for PayPal to side with RedHat,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.340
but RedHat had even more evidence.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.730
He went ahead and called UPS, and UPS would
confirm that the parcel wasn’t even being

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.770
shipped to his home address.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:37.630
What the scammer did do though was ship some
sort of parcel to the same city and state.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:41.709
This way, when you track the package online,
where only the city and state are displayed,

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:44.780
it looks like the package was delivered correctly.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.680
PayPal would take this as evidence that the
package was indeed delivered and close the

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.560
case against RedHat.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.819
All PayPal had to do was call UPS about the
shipment, and it would’ve been clear who

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:55.950
was on the right side.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.659
But, PayPal couldn’t even do that.

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:03.670
While the vast majority of complaints surrounding
PayPal are buyer related, it would be unfair

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.820
to say that it’s the only the buyers that
are getting scammed.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:11.200
The vast majority of PayPal customers are
buyers, and that’s why their stories dominate

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.579
the internet, but there’s plenty of seller
horror stories as well.

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:19.949
One of these stories come from a man named
Andy Dollinger who sells electronics online.

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:23.850
One day, Andy received an inquiry from a customer
regarding if he’s willing to ship products

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.120
to India.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.300
Andy had no problem shipping products internationally,
but he was a bit concerned about the buyer's

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.300
account.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.200
Apparently, the account was created within
the last month, and only had two positive

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.200
feedbacks.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.690
Nonetheless, Andy decided to give the buyer
the benefit of the doubt and shipped him a

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.780
$1,500 iPhone 12 Pro Max.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.039
It took some time for the product to reach
the scammer, but as soon as he received it,

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:50.180
he filed a complaint that the box didn’t
contain the phone.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.449
Andy would reach out to DHL who graciously
provided CCTV footage that proved the weight

00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:55.930
of the box.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:00.150
Andy also contacted his supplier who provided
evidence that the box with the phone weighed

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.199
the same amount, and he turned over both of
these pieces of evidence to PayPal.

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:10.530
At first, it seemed like PayPal actually sided
with Andy, but two weeks later, Andy received

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:14.250
an email letting him know that they had decided
to side with the buyer.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.630
Since then, Andy has called the Indian police,
the European Consumer Center, and sent over

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.660
more evidence to PayPal, but none of this
has gotten Andy his money back.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.080
As you can see, PayPal doesn’t necessarily
always side with the buyer or the seller per

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.070
se, but it does seem like they usually side
with the scammer.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:36.440
The reason for this is that scammers are keenly
aware of exactly how PayPal conducts their

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.319
dispute investigations.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:42.280
And they precisely structure their frauds
to fall through PayPals gaps.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.400
For example, the first scammer knew to send
the product to the same city and state.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.990
And, the second scammer knew to use a new
account without a bad track history.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.240
You would think that PayPal would be aware
of these loopholes in their system, and maybe

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:56.590
they are.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:01.419
But given the raw number of PayPal horror
stories online, it doesn’t seem like they’ve

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:04.229
made any quantifiable change.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:09.979
EXORBITANT FEES:
With such terrible customer service, you would

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:15.210
think that PayPal would atleast charge lower
fees, but this is not the case either.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:20.420
If you’re a buyer or simply sending money
to friends and family, you probably haven’t

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:23.280
noticed this because PayPal is free for you.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.229
But, the same cannot be said about merchants.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:32.280
You see, whenever a customer uses PayPal for
checkout, the merchant has to fork over 3.5%

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.090
+ a fixed fee straight to PayPal.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:40.630
This might be viable for large retailers like
Walmart and Target, but for small merchants,

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:44.360
landlords, and private teachers, this fee
is substantial.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:49.289
For example, let’s say that you’re renting
out your parents old house for $1200 per month.

00:06:49.289 --> 00:06:54.879
At 3.5%, $42 would go straight to PayPal every
single month.

00:06:54.879 --> 00:07:00.400
Through the course of an year, that works
out to over $500, and that’s just for domestic

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.400
transactions.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:05.230
If you’re engaging with international customers
and deal with some sort of currency exchange,

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:08.490
you’re gonna have to fork over a conversion
fee to PayPal as well.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:13.379
By the time international merchants pay all
their fees, they’ve often lost 5% of the

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:15.139
transaction value.

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:19.530
Even credit cards who are known for high fees
have lower fees than that.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:25.569
VISA and Mastercard, for example, charge 1.5
to 2.5% and even American Express only charges

00:07:25.569 --> 00:07:27.690
2.5% to 3.5%.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:31.629
Not to mention, all of these networks provide
fraud protection and customer service that

00:07:31.629 --> 00:07:33.520
are leagues ahead of PayPal.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.210
So, with PayPal, merchants are often forced
to pay higher fees for a worse service that

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:41.310
neither them nor their customers are happy
with.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:45.229
One work around for these fees is to just
complete personal transactions instead of

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:46.880
merchant transactions.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.159
However, this is not only against PayPal’s
terms of service, but most customers are hesitant

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:56.750
to choose this optino especially when it comes
to online transactions with unknown sellers.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:01.930
So, while most sellers would love to ditch
PayPal, they can’t because PayPal is often

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:05.680
the standard and that’s what many customers
are used to.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.410
Fortunately, friendlier options like Zelle
which charge no fees are starting to grow

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:10.410
in popularity.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:15.930
But, in the meantime, sellers can’t do anything
other than be frustrated by PayPal.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:22.000
THE FINANCIAL INSTITUTION THATS NOT:
While customers often cite a wide variety

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.020
of complaints when it comes to PayPal whether
it be scammers, high fees, or frozen funds,

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:31.780
it seems to me that the key reason for almost
all of PayPal’s issues is that they’re

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.200
not regulated like a traditional financial
firm.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.490
One one side, they’re kind of a bank, but
they don’t have to follow the same rules

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.180
as Wells Fargo or Chase.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:45.540
On another side, they’re kind of a payment
network, but they don’t offer the same consumer

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.650
protection services as American Express or
VISA.

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:53.300
Initially, this lack of regulatory oversight
was thanks to PayPal’s unique business model.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:58.150
They were one of the first to jump into the
world of internet finance, so regulators simply

00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.630
didn’t know how to regulate them.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:05.100
PayPal fell into this gray area similar to
crypto brokerages today, and I think we all

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.110
know how that turned out.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.040
Just take a look at Voyager or Celsius.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:15.340
PayPal hasn’t been engaging in anything
anywhere near as risky as those two, and that’s

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.060
why they haven’t completely collapsed.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:22.519
But, that isn’t to say that PayPal’s services
are making anyone particularly happy.

00:09:22.519 --> 00:09:26.540
I mean, there are reasons that there are rules
and regulations surrounding cash reserves,

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:29.720
fraud protection, and fees that traditional
banks must follow.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.860
Throughout the past 100 years, regulators
have figured out that without these rules

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:38.320
and regulations in place, it becomes increasingly
likely that consumers are taken advantage

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:39.320
of.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:43.670
Now, this isn’t to say that PayPal doesn’t
have to follow any rules whatsoever.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:47.459
As regulators better understand the internet,
they have been getting stricter when it comes

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:49.850
to the activities of institutions like PayPal.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:55.490
For example, PayPal has to abide by the following
government regulations: Payments regulation,

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:59.690
banking agency supervision, consumer financial
protection bureau, anti-money laundering,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.880
and interchange fees.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.529
While this is definitely a step in the right
direction, PayPal still doesn’t deal with

00:10:05.529 --> 00:10:09.470
anywhere near as much oversight as traditional
financial institutions.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:13.490
And I’m sure that PayPal’s lawyers and
lobbyists are being paid extremely well to

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.839
make sure that that doesn't change.

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:19.680
And honestly, you can’t even blame them
for that because any business owner would

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:20.910
do the same.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.930
But, that doesn’t mean that this choice
doesn’t have consequences for their customers.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:31.300
THE FUTURE OF PAYPAL:
In the end, while regulation may fix some

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:36.079
of PayPals shortfalls, regulation is rarely
an ultimate solution.

00:10:36.079 --> 00:10:40.530
For PayPal to really turn things around and
improve their reputation and business, change

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.510
has to come from within.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.590
One of the easiest places that they can invest
in is their customer support.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:50.829
It seems that their dispute teams just want
to close cases as fast as possible while following

00:10:50.829 --> 00:10:55.300
PayPal protocol when they’re goal should
be to uncover the truth and side with the

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.529
correct party.

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:02.230
I mean this sounds like a no brainer, but
given all of the stories online, this is obviously

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.070
not what’s taking place.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:08.279
In terms of their fees, while they’re fees
are high, I don’t think PayPal has to reduce

00:11:08.279 --> 00:11:10.030
their fees per se.

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:13.820
Usually, it’s not high fees alone that trigger
customers.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.920
What actually triggers customers is paying
high fees for crappy services.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.450
There are so many businesses that charge high
fees and offer a premium service, and they’re

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:23.649
customers love them.

00:11:23.649 --> 00:11:28.610
I mean one of these companies is literally
the largest company in the world: Apple.

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:32.120
So, PayPal could definitely play this card
as well.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:36.240
But, as long as there’s a gaping disparity
between the cost of PayPal and the perceived

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.930
value of their services, I doubt that PayPal’s
reputation will improve, but that’s just

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:42.930
what I think.

